SKILL:       content
OBJECTIVE:   produce publishable long-form content for itarang.com.
KPIS:        1. Organic clicks to published blog pages — Google Search Console Search Analytics API.
             2. Search impressions for published blog pages — Google Search Console Search Analytics API.
             3. Average search position for published blog pages — Google Search Console Search Analytics API.
             4. First-pass human approval rate — DecisionStore records in approvals.log.
             Run health metrics (not KPIs): Source-fetch success rate — Firecrawl search/scrape result counts; Firecrawl credits per article — Firecrawl /v2/team/credit-usage.
TOOLS:       Firecrawl, Playwright, LinkedIn
PROCEDURE:   1. Confirm that the human-approved content KPI set is recorded for the task; if it is absent, set the task to pending human decision and stop without drafting content.
             2. Confirm Firecrawl is connected before using it; if it is not connected, write blocked — Firecrawl not connected to the task and stop. Use Firecrawl to collect current battery or EV industry news, primary source material, relevant iTarang pages, and existing site coverage required by the assigned title.
             3. When the task calls for iTarang's LinkedIn history, confirm LinkedIn is connected before using it; if it is not connected, write blocked — LinkedIn not connected to the task and stop. Use LinkedIn only as a supporting source because the iTarang post corpus is thin.
             4. Use Playwright, when connected, to verify source pages, publication dates, rendered claims, and the relevant iTarang conversion path when Firecrawl evidence is insufficient. If Playwright is absent, do not block: produce the draft and mark every unverified claim explicitly in the artifact.
             5. Select an iTarang use-case angle, build a source-backed outline, and distinguish verified facts from claims requiring human, product, legal, or technical confirmation.
             6. Choose exactly one publishing category for the article from the six allowed values — financing, battery-selection, charging-maintenance, safety, lifecycle-recycling, partners-industry — based on the decision the article actually helps the reader make, not on the topics it mentions in passing. Emit it as the category front-matter field and record the identical value in the task card's Category field. The category is chosen here, at write time, and is never guessed, defaulted, or inferred at publication time; if no allowed value genuinely fits, set the task to pending human decision and stop rather than picking the closest one.
             7. Draft the long-form article with title, metadata, headings, body, source links, iTarang relevance, and a non-published call to action; do not publish it or modify the live website.
             8. Run a final source, structure, duplication, and claim check, attach the Markdown artifact to the task, and hand it to the CMO review gate.
OUTPUT:      artifacts/<TASK-ID>-content.md — a publishable Markdown long-form article that explains one concept in plain, easy-to-read language; current news may supply evidence or context, but the deliverable is a concept-explaining blog, not a news post. It opens with flat front matter carrying exactly these fields: title, meta_title, meta_description, slug, category, audience, source_urls. The body then supplies the source-backed outline, complete body, cited source URLs and dates, iTarang use-case angle, claims requiring human verification, and proposed internal links and call to action; 900–1,400 words, and a task needing more is split rather than extended. An article whose front matter carries no category, or a category outside the six allowed values, cannot be published: the publisher refuses the article rather than defaulting to one.
CONTRACT:    push to tasks.md as pending CMO review
